extends /views/templates/layouts/_base

append variables
	- pagetitle = "Chart types | Line"
	- pagedescription = "Line charts in Tablechart: a line through the values, or several lines, each named at its end."

block content
	.lane
		.container
			.page-header
				h1 Line
				p.lead A line through the values, or several lines.

	+section("Line chart")
		p Each row of the table is a point. The number is shown above each point.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="line")
				figcaption
					b Rainfall at the orchard
					br
					span millimetres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '84'], ['Feb', '71'], ['Mar', '76'], ['Apr', '58'], ['May', '61'], ['Jun', '47'], ['Jul', '39'], ['Aug', '52']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Smooth")
		p If the figure has #[code data-chart-shape="smooth"], the line is a curve through every point. It never goes higher or lower than the points on either side of it, so the curve shows no peak or dip that is not in the data, and a flat stretch stays flat.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-shape="smooth")
				figcaption
					b Rainfall at the orchard
					br
					span millimetres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '84'], ['Feb', '71'], ['Mar', '76'], ['Apr', '58'], ['May', '61'], ['Jun', '47'], ['Jul', '39'], ['Aug', '52']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If you want to make every line and area smooth, set the #[code shape] option to #[code "smooth"]. Then #[code data-chart-shape="straight"] makes one chart straight again.

	+section("Grow")
		p If the figure has #[code data-chart-animate="grow"], the line grows from zero instead of from left to right. The points move with it, and the numbers appear when it is done.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-shape="smooth" data-chart-animate="grow")
				figcaption
					b Rainfall at the orchard
					br
					span millimetres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '84'], ['Feb', '71'], ['Mar', '76'], ['Apr', '58'], ['May', '61'], ['Jun', '47'], ['Jul', '39'], ['Aug', '52']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If you want to make every line and area grow, set the #[code animate] option to #[code "grow"]. A chart with triggered steps (like from Reveal.js fragments) still builds from left to right. See #[a(href=url('/styling/animation.html')) Animation].

	+section("Below zero")
		p A line can cross zero. A number below zero is shown under its point, and a grey line marks zero.

		+chart(null, '7-8,10')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-shape="smooth" data-chart-animate="grow")
				figcaption
					b Monthly result
					br
					span thousands of euros
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '12.6'], ['Feb', '28.7'], ['Mar', '-11.7'], ['Apr', '-36.3'], ['May', '19.8'], ['Jun', '-4.1']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Several lines")
		p Each value column is a line. The column header is the name of the line, shown at the end of the line. If the lines end close together, and their names and values crowd each other there, show the names in a legend instead: see #[a(href=url('/features/legends.html')) Legends].

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="line")
				figcaption: b Apples per tree
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30'], ['2019', '58', '39'], ['2022', '77', '52'], ['2025', '105', '61']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p The numbers depend on the number of lines:
		ul
			li One line: the number is above each point.
			li Two lines: the number of the higher point is above it, the number of the lower point below it.
			li Three lines or more: if there is room, the number is above its point. If not, it is below it. If there is no room there either, the number is left out, and if you point at a point, or tap it, a tooltip shows the numbers of that row. If one line is highlighted, only that line shows its numbers.

	+section("Points")
		p With several lines, each line has its own point, in this order: a filled circle, an open circle, a filled square, an open square, a filled diamond and an open diamond. A seventh line starts again with the filled circle. The legend shows the same points.

		+chart
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend)
				figcaption: b Apples per tree
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th South
							th East
							th West
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30', '22', '12'], ['2019', '58', '39', '35', '20'], ['2022', '77', '52', '41', '33'], ['2025', '105', '61', '70', '45']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]
								td= row[4]

		p If you want another point on a line, add #[code data-marker] to its column header: #[code circle], #[code square] or #[code diamond]. If you want an open point, add #[code open], such as #[code data-marker="diamond open"].

		+chart(null, '4')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend)
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th(data-marker="diamond") North
							th(data-marker="diamond open") South
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30'], ['2019', '58', '39'], ['2022', '77', '52'], ['2025', '105', '61']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]

		p An open point is cut out of the line, so the background shows through it.

	+section("Dashed and dotted lines")
		p If the column header of a line has #[code data-line="dashed"] or #[code data-line="dotted"], the line is dashed or dotted. The legend shows the same line.

		+chart(null, '5')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-legend)
				figcaption: b Apples per tree
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th North
							th(data-line="dashed") South
							th(data-line="dotted") East
							th West
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30', '22', '12'], ['2019', '58', '39', '35', '20'], ['2022', '77', '52', '41', '33'], ['2025', '105', '61', '70', '45']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]
								td= row[4]

		p #[code --tablechart-series-dash] and #[code --tablechart-series-dot] set the pattern, in pixels: #[code 6 4] and #[code 0 4] by default.
		p If you want to give each line its own colour, use a #[a(href=url('/styling/colours.html')) palette].

	+section("Highlight a line")
		p If the header of a line has #[code data-kind="focus"], that line keeps its colour and shows its numbers. The other lines are shown fainter.

		+chart(null, '4')
			figure(data-chart="line")
				table
					thead
						tr
							th Year
							th(data-kind="focus") North
							th South
							th East
					tbody
						each row in [['2016', '41', '30', '22'], ['2019', '58', '39', '35'], ['2022', '77', '52', '41'], ['2025', '105', '61', '70']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]
								td= row[2]
								td= row[3]

	+section("Steps")
		p If the figure has #[code data-chart-shape="step"], each value is a flat line across the width of its row, and the line goes straight up or down to the next row. A step line has no points. A number is shown only where the value changes, in the middle of the rows that have that value.

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-shape="step")
				figcaption
					b Price of a bag of apples
					br
					span euros
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '2.40'], ['Feb', '2.40'], ['Mar', '2.60'], ['Apr', '2.60'], ['May', '2.60'], ['Jun', '2.90'], ['Jul', '2.90'], ['Aug', '2.70']]
							tr
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Stacked lines")
		p If the figure has #[code data-chart-stack], each line starts on top of the one before it. For stacked lines, an #[a(href=url('/types/area.html')) area chart] is usually easier to read.

	+section("Missing values")
		p If a cell has no number, such as #[code n/a], the line has a gap there.

		+chart(null, '6')
			figure(data-chart="line")
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '88'], ['Feb', '74'], ['Mar', 'n/a'], ['Apr', '49'], ['May', '55']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

	+section("Many rows")
		-
			const months = ['January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June', 'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December'];
			// Three years of apple juice sold: more each year, most in winter.
			const juice = Array.from({ length: 36 }, (_, i) => [
				`${months[i % 12]} ${2023 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`,
				`${months[i % 12].slice(0, 3)} ’${23 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`,
				(20 + i * 0.35 + 4 * Math.cos(((i % 12) / 6) * Math.PI) + ((i * 7) % 5) * 0.4).toFixed(1),
			]);
		p A chart with many rows has little room for each number and name. Tablechart then shows fewer of them:

		ul
			li If the values of a line would touch, only a few are shown: the first, the last, the highest, the lowest and the values far from the trend. If you point at a row, or tap it, a tooltip shows its value. See #[a(href='#which-values-are-shown') Which values are shown].
			li If the names under the chart do not fit, they get smaller, down to 70%. If they still do not fit, only every second, third or more is shown, evenly spaced, at full size. A name at an end moves in, so it stays inside the chart.
			li Each name has a tick above it, at the place of its row, so you can see which point it belongs to, also when it moved in. They have the colour and width of the gridlines, a little stronger. #[code --tablechart-tick-color], #[code --tablechart-tick-opacity] and #[code --tablechart-tick-width] change them. #[code data-chart-ticks="false"], or the #[code ticks] option, turns the ticks off.
			li A point is shown where a value is. If all values are shown and the points would touch each other, the line has no points.

		p A short name under the chart needs less room. A row can have its full name in the table, which a screen reader and the tooltip read, and a short name for under the chart in #[code data-short].

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="line")
					figcaption
						b Apple juice sold
						br
						span thousands of litres per month
					table
						tbody
							each row in juice
								tr
									th(data-short=row[1])= row[0]
									td= row[2]

			+codeblock('HTML', null, '4-5').
				<figure data-chart="line">
					<table>
						<tbody>
							<tr><th data-short="#{juice[0][1]}">#{juice[0][0]}</th><td>#{juice[0][2]}</td></tr>
							<tr><th data-short="#{juice[1][1]}">#{juice[1][0]}</th><td>#{juice[1][2]}</td></tr>
							<!-- …one row per month, 36 in all -->
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</figure>

		p A row with a translation key, such as #[code juice-row11], gets the key #[code juice-row11-short] for its short name under the chart. A translation script then needs a short name for that key too.
		p For a long table, a summary can say in a few sentences what a listener needs, such as the highest value and the trend. See #[a(href=url('/features/summary.html')) Summary].

	+section("Which values are shown")
		-
			const names = ['January', 'February', 'March', 'April', 'May', 'June', 'July', 'August', 'September', 'October', 'November', 'December'];
			// Three years of apple juice sold, with a new apple press from March 2024.
			const pressed = Array.from({ length: 36 }, (_, i) => [
				`${names[i % 12]} ${2023 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`,
				`${names[i % 12].slice(0, 3)} ’${23 + Math.floor(i / 12)}`,
				(20 + i * 0.35 + 4 * Math.cos(((i % 12) / 6) * Math.PI) + ((i * 7) % 5) * 0.4).toFixed(1),
				i === 14,
			]);
		p A value for every row is too much on a line with many rows. With #[code data-chart-values], or the #[code values] option for all charts, you choose which values a line or an area chart shows:

		table
			thead
				tr
					th Value
					th Shows
			tbody
				tr
					td: code auto
					td All values if they fit, and else the first, the last, the highest, the lowest and the outliers. The default.
				tr
					td: code all
					td All values. If they would touch, every second, third or more.
				tr
					td: code none
					td No values. With #[code data-chart-axis], the values can be read along the axis.
				tr
					td: code first last highest lowest outliers
					td Only these, any of them, in any order. #[code outliers] are the values more than 25% from the trend, as in a #[a(href=url('/features/summary.html#trend-and-outliers')) summary].
				tr
					td: code auto last
					td All values if they fit, and else only the ones after #[code auto].

		p A row with #[code data-value="show"] always shows its value, such as the month a new press came into use. A point is shown where a value is. Each value goes above, below or beside its point, where it is clear of the lines, the names and the other values; a value at a dip tries below first. If there is no such place, it may cross a line, which then stops under the value, but it never covers another value, a name or a point. If two values would still touch, the one that matters less is left out, with its point: first a row with #[code data-value="show"] is kept, then the last value, the first, the highest, the lowest and the outliers.

		.card
			.demo
				figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-values="auto last")
					figcaption
						b Apple juice sold
						br
						span thousands of litres per month
					table
						tbody
							each row in pressed
								tr(data-value=row[3] ? 'show' : false)
									th(data-short=row[1])= row[0]
									td= row[2]

			+codeblock('HTML', null, '1,5').
				<figure data-chart="line" data-chart-values="auto last">
					<table>
						<tbody>
							<tr><th data-short="Jan ’23">January 2023</th><td>#{pressed[0][2]}</td></tr>
							<tr data-value="show"><th data-short="Mar ’24">March 2024</th><td>#{pressed[14][2]}</td></tr>
							<!-- …one row per month, 36 in all -->
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</figure>

		p A value that is not shown is in the tooltip of its row, and in the table for screen readers. In print, #[code data-chart-table="auto"] prints the table, or the summary if the table is long. See #[a(href=url('/features/summary.html')) Summary].

	+section("Axis and gridlines")
		p By default there is no value axis: the numbers on the points show the values. If you want numbers along the axis, on the left, add #[code data-chart-axis]. If you want a light line at each of those numbers, add #[code data-chart-grid]. You can add one of them, or both. If you want to add them to every chart, set the #[code axis] and #[code grid] options to #[code true].

		+chart(null, '1')
			figure(data-chart="line" data-chart-axis data-chart-grid)
				figcaption
					b Rainfall at the orchard
					br
					span millimetres
				table
					tbody
						each row in [['Jan', '84'], ['Feb', '71'], ['Mar', '76'], ['Apr', '58'], ['May', '61'], ['Jun', '47'], ['Jul', '39'], ['Aug', '52']]
							tr(data-kind=row[2])
								th= row[0]
								td= row[1]

		p If a chart has an axis or gridlines, its scale ends on a number of the axis. If you want to set the scale yourself, add #[code data-chart-scale], such as #[code data-chart-scale="0 100"]. If you want to change how the axis and gridlines look, see #[a(href=url('/styling/axes.html')) Axes and gridlines].

	+section("Next")
		p If you want to show the names in a row under the chart, see #[a(href=url('/features/legends.html')) Legends].
